            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"An idiom for extreme danger, mortal peril, profound gloom, or (in prophetic/NT contexts) the spiritual darkness and bondage of humanity apart from God.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A typological outline or pattern of sin, darkness, and bondage that leads to the grave, functioning as the structural inverse to the 'Shadow of Christ' (His appointed times and feasts).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Mortal peril, deep darkness, or spiritual lostness.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The image, outline, or boundary of death, expressed as a systemic pattern of sin and bondage that opposes God's appointed times.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book discards the primary consensus application of circumstantial gloom, mortal danger, or the experience of dying (e.g., Psalm 23:4), redefining all instances as a systemic typological pattern of sin opposed to biblical feasts.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus understands 'shadow of death' as a unified idiom for intense darkness or danger (often rooted in the Hebrew 'tsalmaveth' as deep darkness). The book splits the phrase conceptually, interpreting 'shadow' as a typological outline or image (akin to Colossians 2:17) to frame the term as a structural system of sin.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By defining 'shadow' as an 'image, outline, or boundary' and explicitly contrasting the phrase with Christ's appointed feasts, the book replaces the accepted idiomatic meaning of extreme darkness and peril with a highly specific, idiosyncratic typological construct.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Hebrew term 'tsalmaveth' is an idiom for profound darkness or mortal peril. Applying a typological definition of 'shadow' (an outline of things to come, as used in the New Testament Greek 'skia') to this Hebrew idiom forces an English/Greek conceptual pun onto texts like Psalm 23:4, distorting their original contextual meaning.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
